---
intent: >
  Add a tenth `brain` category to aide_upgrade — detection-only via
  parseBrainAide. Reports matches/missing/malformed for
  .aide/config/brain.aide; never writes the file. Apply mode for
  category=brain returns an `instructions` field routing the agent to the
  `/aide:brain config` skill, which is the single canonical home for
  brain.aide creation.
---

## Project Structure

The tree below shows the proposed state of `src/tools/upgrade/` after this plan executes. All file additions are the brain detection block in the orchestrator and the brain branch in `applyFiles` — no new files. The plan is purely additive within existing files.

```
src/tools/upgrade/
├── .aide                              ← intent spec for upgrade orchestrator (existing, just amended with tenth category)
├── plan.aide                          ← this plan (new — overwrites prior 10/10 plan)
├── index.ts                           ← orchestrator (modified): adds brain to UpgradeInput.category enum,
│                                        adds brain detection block calling parseBrainAide, appends brain
│                                        category to assembled categories array. Signature unchanged:
│                                        upgrade(root: string, framework?: FrameworkType, path?: string)
│                                        => Promise<UpgradeResult>. The buildCategoryResult helper is
│                                        reused as-is — no new helper introduced for the brain category.
├── index.test.ts                      ← orchestrator tests (modified): mocks parseBrainAide, adds tests
│                                        for the four brain branches (ok / missing-file / malformed-frontmatter
│                                        / malformed-body) and the apply-mode brain branch. Existing tests
│                                        bump categories.length expectation from 9 to 10.
├── applyFiles/
│   ├── index.ts                       ← apply helper (modified): adds early-return brain branch BEFORE
│   │                                    the existing matches→unchanged mapping. On status missing or
│   │                                    malformed for category brain, return the file with an
│   │                                    instructions field; never writes. Default export signature
│   │                                    unchanged: applyFiles(files: UpgradeFileResult[])
│   │                                    => Promise<UpgradeFileResult[]>.
│   └── index.test.ts                  ← apply tests (existing): add three test cases — brain matches
│                                        passes through as unchanged; brain missing returns instructions
│                                        with no disk write; brain malformed returns instructions with
│                                        no disk write. mkdir/writeFile must remain unmocked-as-called
│                                        for these branches.
├── buildVersionsMeta/                 ← (existing, untouched)
├── checkIdeConfig/                    ← (existing, untouched)
├── checkMcpConfig/                    ← (existing, untouched)
├── compareFile/                       ← (existing, untouched)
└── spliceStub/                        ← (existing, untouched)
```

## Prerequisites

Glue touches outside `src/tools/upgrade/` that this plan depends on. Each is a small, named edit — not a redesign of the target module. The implementor performs them as part of the indicated steps.

- `src/types/index.ts` must extend the `UpgradeCategory` union with `"brain"`. The union becomes:
  `"pointer-stub" | "methodology-docs" | "version-metadata" | "commands" | "agents" | "skills" | "mcp" | "ide" | "readme" | "brain"`.
  No other type changes required — `UpgradeFileResult` already supports `instructions: string` (used by IDE-VS-Code precedent) and the `summary` shape already has `total / matches / missing` which is all the brain category produces.

- `src/index.ts` must update the `aide_upgrade` MCP tool registration:
  - Add `"brain"` to the `category` enum on `inputSchema.properties.category`.
  - Append `brain` to the comma-separated category list in the tool description prose ("Categories: pointer-stub, methodology-docs, …, readme, **brain**").
  - Add one sentence to the tool description body documenting the detection-only contract: brain is the only category whose apply-mode call never writes — instead the manifest entry carries an `instructions` field directing the agent to invoke `/aide:brain config`.
  No handler dispatch changes — apply-mode for category=brain rides through the existing `applyFiles` path, and `applyFiles` carries the new brain branch.

- `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` must already export the default `parseBrainAide(root: string) => Promise<ParseBrainAideResult>`. This already exists in the codebase — the plan consumes it; it does not modify it.

## Plan

### 1. Extend the `UpgradeCategory` type union

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Add `"brain"` as the tenth member of the `UpgradeCategory` exported union in `src/types/index.ts`. Place it last to mirror the order in the spec's outcome bullet ("the ten-category vocabulary (`pointer-stub`, `methodology-docs`, `version-metadata`, `commands`, `agents`, `skills`, `mcp`, `ide`, `readme`, `brain`)") and the order in the canonical list in `src/index.ts`. Do not modify any other type. The `UpgradeFileStatus` union already supports `matches`, `missing`, and `malformed`; the `UpgradeFileResult` interface already supports an optional `instructions` field — both are reused as-is. Traced to: `.aide` outcomes.desired bullet on the ten-category vocabulary; spec body "the ten-category vocabulary".

### 2. Add the brain detection block to the orchestrator

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

- [x] 2a. In `src/tools/upgrade/index.ts`, import the default export from `@/service/parseBrainAide/index.js` at the top of the file, alongside the existing service imports (`detectFramework`, `readCanonicalDoc`, etc.). The import name is `parseBrainAide`.

- [x] 2b. In the same file, extend the `UpgradeInput` zod schema's `category` enum to include `"brain"` as the tenth value. Order must mirror the type union order from step 1. The `.describe(...)` text on `category` does not need to change — it is generic.

- [x] 2c. After the `// ── i. README` block (the README result assembly) and before the `// ── Assemble categories` block, add a new section comment-block `// ── j. Brain config ──` and the brain detection logic. The block:
  - Computes the brain.aide host path as `join(projectRoot, ".aide", "config", "brain.aide")` for the `filePath` field on the result. Use `join` from the existing `node:path` import.
  - Calls `await parseBrainAide(projectRoot)` once.
  - Maps the `parseBrainAide` tagged result to a single `UpgradeFileResult` value with `category: "brain"`, `name: ".aide/config/brain.aide"`, and the brain.aide host path as `filePath`. The status mapping is:
    - `kind: "ok"` → status `"matches"`
    - `kind: "missing"` → status `"missing"`
    - any other kind (`"malformed-frontmatter"`, `"malformed-body"`) → status `"malformed"`
  - Never sets `canonicalContent` for any branch — there is no canonical template for this category (the brain root path is host-specific).
  - Never sets `prescription` — that field is mcp-only.
  - Never sets `instructions` here — the orchestrator is read-only; instructions are added in apply mode by `applyFiles` (step 4).

- [x] 2d. In the `// ── Assemble categories` block at the bottom of the same function, append `buildCategoryResult("brain", [brainResult])` as the final entry of the `categories` array, after `buildCategoryResult("readme", [readmeFileResult])`. Do not introduce a new helper; reuse the existing `buildCategoryResult` exactly — its current `summary` shape (`total`, `differs`, `matches`, `missing`) handles the brain category natively because malformed files count as zero `differs`/zero `matches`/zero `missing` in the auto-summary, and the per-file `status` carries the precise `malformed` distinction. Traced to: spec's apply-mapping reference (delegation note's third Decisions bullet).

