## Decisions

**1. Reuse `parseBrainAide`, do not introduce a new helper folder under `src/tools/upgrade/`.**
The plan adds zero new files inside `src/tools/upgrade/`. The brain detection is a single function call against the existing `parseBrainAide` service. Wrapping it in `src/tools/upgrade/checkBrain/index.ts` would add a folder whose entire body is one line of dispatch — an extra hop with no value. Folder-structure escalation rule: a single-consumer dispatch belongs inline in its consumer. The block sits next to the parallel inline blocks in `index.ts` (a–i) and follows the same pattern. Traced to: `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure` (Shared Submodules — single consumer stays in that operation's folder); existing pattern of inline `// ── h. IDE config` and `// ── i. README` blocks in `index.ts`.

**2. No `differs` state for the brain category.**
The brain root path is host-specific (inlined verbatim into `mcpServerConfig.args` with no placeholder), so byte comparison against a canonical template is structurally meaningless. The category produces only `matches`, `missing`, and `malformed`. The summary's existing `differs: 0` count is correct by construction; no schema change is needed. Traced to: spec outcomes.desired bullet on the three-value vocabulary; spec outcomes.undesired bullet "An upgrade that byte-compares … and reports `differs`".

**3. Malformed files count toward `missing` in the summary numerator (no fourth count field).**
The existing `summary` shape has `total / differs / matches / missing` (plus optional `updated / created / unchanged` for apply mode). Adding a fourth count field for `malformed` would force every other category to declare it too. Instead, the per-file `status` field carries the precise malformed distinction; the summary auto-counts malformed files as zero in every existing bucket, and consumers of the summary read the per-file status when they need to disambiguate. The `malformed` status bucket is left at zero in the summary intentionally — it is not a count the agent uses to drive the wizard; the wizard branches on whether ANY file in the category needs attention, which `differs + missing > 0` already captures (and a malformed brain file shows up in `missing` semantically: "you need to do something about this"). The detection mapping in the delegation note allows this read explicitly. Traced to: detection mapping reference.

**4. The orchestrator stays read-only; the `instructions` field is set in apply mode only.**
Same architectural rule as the existing IDE-VS-Code precedent. The orchestrator (`index.ts`) computes statuses and content; `applyFiles` (in apply mode, called from the handler) decides whether to write or instruct. This keeps the orchestrator JSDoc invariant ("read-only — never writes files") true. Traced to: spec Strategy "The tool is a comparator; the agent is the executor"; existing applyFiles dispatch precedent (IDE VS Code branch lines 56–60).

**5. `applyFiles` brain branch goes BEFORE `matches → unchanged`, but the runtime ordering is a no-op for brain matches.**
Conceptually, the brain branch handles only `missing` and `malformed`. The existing `matches → unchanged` branch handles `matches` for every category including brain. Either physical order works for `matches` because the brain branch's status guard excludes it. The plan places the brain branch BEFORE `matches → unchanged` for symmetry with the existing IDE-VS-Code branch, which is also placed early in the dispatch ladder for the "skip disk write" cases. Traced to: existing dispatch order in `applyFiles/index.ts` (early-return for skip-disk cases).

**6. The MCP tool description gets one new sentence and one enum entry — nothing more.**
The outcomes.desired bullet on the description-as-auditable-contract makes any drift between description and handler a falsifiable QA failure. The minimum description change that captures the brain category's contract is: (a) add it to the categories list; (b) add it to the enum; (c) add one sentence on the detection-only / `/aide:brain config` routing. Adding more prose risks teaching expectations the handler does not implement; adding less drops the brain category from the "auditable contract surface." Traced to: spec outcomes.desired bullet "The MCP tool description registered for `aide_upgrade` … is an auditable contract surface, not commentary"; spec outcomes.undesired bullet on description-vs-handler / description-vs-spec drift.

**7. The `instructions` string is fixed prose, not assembled.**
`"Run /aide:brain config to set up the brain."` — short, imperative, names the canonical home. The wording does not vary by `missing` vs `malformed`; both terminal statuses route to the same skill, which has its own internal branching for "create new" vs "fix existing." Keeping the instructions string identical across both kinds avoids encoding two parallel remediation flows that the skill itself owns. Traced to: spec outcomes.desired bullet "the manifest entry routes the agent to `/aide:brain config`, which is the single canonical home"; spec outcomes.undesired bullet "An upgrade that scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` from inside the `brain` category — duplicating the brain-root-path interview".

**8. No edit to `.claude/commands/aide/upgrade.md`.**
Per project policy stated in the delegation: `.claude/commands/`, `.claude/agents/`, `.claude/skills/` are agent harness — direct edits, not pipelined. The orchestrator-level slash command will be edited outside this pipeline once the engine-side support for the brain category is in place. The plan deliberately stops at the engine boundary. Traced to: delegation's "Out of scope" section.
